**How to Create a Program in C#**

Are you interested in learning how to create a program in C#? Do you want to know how to get started with this popular programming language? Look no further! This article will guide you through the process of creating a simple program in C# using Visual C# 2010 Express Edition.

**Step 1: Download and Install Visual C# 2010 Express Edition**

Go here to download your free copy of Visual C# 2010 Express Edition. Run the downloaded executable and follow these steps:

**Step 2: Run Visual C# 2010 Express Edition**

Next, run Visual C# 2010 Express Edition.

**Step 3: Create a New Project**

Go to File → New → Project.

**Step 4: Select the Project Type**

Select Visual C# → Windows → Console Application.

**Step 5: Name Your Project**

Press OK. You should see this:

```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;
namespace ConsoleApplication1
{
  class Program
  {
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
    }
  }
}
```

**Step 6: Write Your Code**

Beneath `static void Main(string[] args)`, after the first curly brace, type:

```
Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
```

It should look like this:

```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;
namespace ConsoleApplication1
{
  class Program
  {
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
      Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
    }
  }
}
```

**Step 7: Run Your Program**

Click the Run button on the toolbar. Congratulations!

**Alternative Method: Compiling C# under Linux**

If you're running Linux, you need CVS and GNU build tools. Go to the DotGNU project (http://www.gnu.org/software/dotgnu/) that provides FOSS implementation of C#. You can choose to get the source code and build your C# environment from scratch or you may try pre-compiled distributions first. Try to start some examples that also come in precompiled (.exe) form.

**Edit Your C# Code**

Under Linux, you can use KWrite or gedit to edit the C# code - the recent versions of both editors support the syntax highlight for this language.

